{"objective":"Make the performance and durability mechanisms do what they are named for, and reduce the structural concentration that makes change risky in the least-tested packages.","scope":"1. Decide code-graph freshness from cheap filesystem metadata so a cache hit parses nothing and a miss builds once. 2. Raise coverage where blast radius is highest — metadata, runtime, store — and report per-package coverage in CI so regressions are visible rather than averaged away. 3. Decompose the MCP tool dispatcher per tool or per risk class with no behavioural change, keeping the single-source catalog the only place a tool is declared. Exit criterion: a second code-context call on an unchanged tree performs no parse, high-risk packages show coverage comparable to the best-covered package, and the dispatcher is reviewable per tool with the existing suite passing unmodified.","outOfScope":"Not in this wave: any change to the store's concurrency model or to the SQLite driver, package boundary restructuring in eka-core, and performance work on the canonical store itself. The snapshot-swap crash window is acknowledged but not addressed here because the current sequence is documented and the loss window is narrow; it belongs with a broader durability pass."}